ratch, and they would win only one medal at the 1964 Winter Olympics, even that somewhat of a surprise."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The three standard events – men’s, ladies’, and pairs – were held indoors at the Olympic Ice Stadium. Men and women skated compulsory figures and a free skating program, with the compulsory figures counting towards 60% of the final score, while pairs skated a single free skate program. Overall placements were determined by the majority placement system. This was the last international competition in which pairs skating consisted of only a single program. The 1963 and 1964 European Championships had seen the pairs skate a compulsory and free program, and at the 1964 World Championships, held shortly after the Innsbruck Olympics, this would become the standard. By 1968, the Olympic pairs program would consist of two phases, later to be renamed the short and long program. Controversy enveloped the medal standings in the 1964 pairs program, and it would not be resolved until the 1980s (some participants would say it is still not resolved) – details can be found in the description of the pairs event."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Events"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped"} "\n" [:thead {} "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Event"] "\n" [:th {} "Status"] "\n" [:th {} "Date"] "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:th {} "NOCs"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/13112"} "Singles, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "3 –  6 February 1964"] "\n" [:td {} "24"] "\n" [:td {} "11"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/13142"} "Singles, Women"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "30 January –  2 February 1964"] "\n" [:td {} "30"] "\n" [:td {} "14"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/13172"} "Pairs, Mixed"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "29 January 1964"] "\n" [:td {} "34"] "\n" [:td {} "7"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {}] "\n" [:td {}] "\n" [:td {}] "\n" [:td {:class "border-top"